WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.520
Model Context Protocol security is about protecting the integration path between AI

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.000
applications and the outside systems they can reach.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.975
MCP makes it easier for an AI host to connect to tools, resources, prompts, data stores,

00:00:11.975 --> 00:00:13.080
and services.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.000
That usefulness changes the security boundary.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.320
A model response is no longer only text on a screen; it may lead to a tool call,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.000
a file read, an API request, a ticket update, or another action in a real environment.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
At a high level, an MCP host is the AI application or environment where the user works.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.985
An MCP client is the component that speaks to an MCP server.

00:00:31.985 --> 00:00:34.000
The server exposes tools, resources, or prompts.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.015
A tool is an action the system can call.

00:00:36.015 --> 00:00:38.960
A resource is data the system can read or present.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.440
Prompt templates can shape how tasks are framed.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.985
Transports carry messages.

00:00:42.985 --> 00:00:46.950
Downstream systems are the APIs, files, databases, cloud services,

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.985
and workflow platforms that ultimately provide data or perform work.

00:00:50.985 --> 00:00:52.895
MCP security is not only model security.

00:00:52.895 --> 00:00:57.000
Prompt wording can help guide behavior, but it is not a reliable security boundary.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.030
The important controls live across application security, identity, authorization,

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:05.030
data protection, tool governance, supply-chain review, logging, monitoring,

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.000
and operations.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.255
A secure design asks what the model can see, what tools it can call,

00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:12.000
which identity those calls use, what data can move,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.235
and what actions can change real systems.

00:01:14.235 --> 00:01:20.960
Useful MCP systems must be bounded, authorized, observable, and recoverable.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:21.000
Bounded means tools and resources have clear limits.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.980
Authorized means permissions come from policy and identity, not from model confidence.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.900
Observable means tool calls, denials, approvals, and resource access can be investigated.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.980
Recoverable means high-impact actions have rollback plans and incident response paths.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.020
The goal is not to block MCP adoption;

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.930
it is to make powerful integrations safe enough to operate.
